 two cut lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"two-cut-lengths":["cnm_139","cnm_038","cnm_091","cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_038","cnm_091","cnm_089","cnm_069","cnm_055","cnm_036","cnm_044"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 12 cards","reason":"If N is the original total, the unchanged red count gives N/3 = (N+4)/4. Thus 4N = 3N+12 and N = 12; the red count is 4 both before and after the addition.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equate-red-counts","label":"Equate the unchanged red counts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equate-red-counts":["cnm_p146","cnm_044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p146","cnm_044"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (1:25 PM)","reason":"The first three workers work for 105 minutes and pack (4+3+3)·(105/3) = 350 packages. Daria packs the remaining 100, requiring (100/5)·4 = 80 minutes. Eighty minutes before 2:45 PM is 1:25 PM.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"linear-work-equation","label":"Solve for Daria's working time","methodDirectNodeIds":{"linear-work-equation":["cnm_046","cnm_044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_046","cnm_044"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"5","reason":"Set a = 2k, b = 3k, c = 5k. Then 2a + 3b - c = (4 + 9 - 5)k = 8k = 20, so k = 5/2 and a = 5. Substituting b = 3a/2 and c = 5a/2 also gives 4a = 20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_268_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"direct-substitution","label":"Substitute in terms of a","methodDirectNodeIds":{"direct-substitution":["cnm_093","cnm_089","cnm_044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_093","cnm_089","cnm_044"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, one value of n","reason":"The equation 15+(n-20)/3=n/2 gives n=50. The remaining 30 questions contribute 10 correct answers, giving 25/50=50%, so exactly one value works.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_027","cnm_042","cnm_p195"],"id":"total-correct-equation","label":"Count the correct answers in two ways","methodDirectNodeIds":{"total-correct-equation":["cnm_045","cnm_044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_045","cnm_044","cnm_027","cnm_042","cnm_p195"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 20","reason":"The mode is 2 and the mean is (25+x)/7. For x<=2, the median equals the mode, which is impossible for a nonconstant progression. For 2<x<4 the median is x, and for x>=4 it is 4. The valid solutions are x=3 and x=17, producing statistics (2,3,4) and (2,4,6). Their sum is 20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2000_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_044","cnm_096"],"id":"median-cases","label":"Separate the possible median positions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-cases":["cnm_211","cnm_213","cnm_h203"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_211","cnm_213","cnm_h203","cnm_044","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1:36 P.M.","reason":"After t hours, the remaining fractions are 1-t/3 and 1-t/4. Solving 1-t/4=2(1-t/3) gives t=12/5 hours, or 2 hours 24 minutes, so lighting must occur at 1:36 P.M.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_027","cnm_043","cnm_p062","cnm_p063"],"id":"remaining-length-equation","label":"Compare the remaining lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remaining-length-equation":["cnm_045","cnm_044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_045","cnm_044","cnm_027","cnm_043","cnm_p062","cnm_p063"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, BF=4.2","reason":"Write h=BF. Similarity gives AF=5h/3 and CF=5h/7. Since AC=AE+EC=10, 5h/3+5h/7=10, yielding h=21/5=4.2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1990_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_116","cnm_060","cnm_055","cnm_044"],"id":"two_similar_triangle_pairs","label":"Express both parts of AC using BF","methodDirectNodeIds":{"two_similar_triangle_pairs":["cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_116","cnm_060","cnm_055","cnm_044"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 25%","reason":"Let t be the total after adding water. Then (t/5+1)/(t+1)=1/3, giving t=5. The original mixture therefore contained 1 ounce of acid in 4 ounces total, or 25%.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1973_AMC_12_Q33","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p194","cnm_p195","cnm_027"],"id":"one-total-variable","label":"Model the total after adding water","methodDirectNodeIds":{"one-total-variable":["cnm_p197","cnm_p196","cnm_045","cnm_044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p197","cnm_p196","cnm_045","cnm_044","cnm_p194","cnm_p195","cnm_027"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_044","status":"mapped","total":46}
